      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "> I think it is necessary to generate IL code dynamically in advance instead of using the interpreter.\nThis game is currently compiled using il2cpp, so at the end the game client is mostly native cpp. So its a lot more complicated. \nBut the real question still should be: why did vrchat team decide to write own simple VM like that for udon.\nAs other alternative solutions:\n- Use existing VM-s, like lua, on desktop lua-jit could be used. And udon graphs could be just compiled to it. Lua can be used in safe sandboxed mode with whitelist of available methods, so it would be safe and would be still easy and simple to disallow access to some components/objects on scene. Pros: there is shit tons of existing resources for lua, would make developing much easier and faster. Cons: Can't find a single one.\n- Compile udon bytecode to native code on backend. Pros: could be made very fast. Cons: Hard to implement + cost.\n- Compiling to IL should be also possible with some additional tricks... as c# runtime can talk with cpp code, but I have no idea if there are any existing tools for unity to create such mixed environment, with both il2cpp + additional code in pure c#/il.\nAs otherwise I dont believe that udon VM will ever get fast enough, as fast as other languages developed by much larger teams with a lot of experience.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
